id: core/entity-and-coupling
title: "entities and the coupling between them"
kind: concept
record_shape: header
status: draft
authored_by: agent-on-behalf
authored_via: "Claude Code session on Dave's behalf, 2026-09-12; from Dave's spoken description, not separately ratified"
updated: 2026-09-12

purpose: >
  Name the entities this protocol coordinates, give each its address, and say
  what the slash between two of them means. The slash is the relationship: the
  coupling where interactions live, kept separate from the observer on either
  side of it. This is the move the protocol is built on and it was never written
  down as one record.

entities:
  principal:
    name: Dave
    ref: principal:dave
    role: >
      Observer and root principal. Sits at the top of the holarchy: the questions
      answered at this level set the questions the bots and child Yawns below it
      get to work on. Owns meaning, values, consent, and ratification.
    role_settled:
      what_am_i_to_be: "steward of yawn.bot"
      why: >
        So that Dave stays flexible and agile with as much agency as possible,
        while the bills are paid and leverage is as high as it can be.
      settled_on: 2026-09-12
      epistemic_status: reported
      recall_rule: >
        When the role question resurfaces — Dave asks it again, or a bot detects
        the pattern of it — answer FROM THIS RECORD first: "Last time we checked,
        what you are to be is the steward of yawn.bot, so that…" and attach the
        why. Do not reopen the question unless Dave corrects the answer. The
        question keeps its key (role) but drops in rank once answered; the top
        slot goes to the next highest-leverage question in
        records/yawn.bot-state.yawn.
  runtime:
    name: yawn.bot
    ref: agents/yawn.bot.yawn
    role: >
      The bounded runtime. Root steward inside the contract; may propose,
      mirror, infer, and ask; may not ratify, activate itself, spend, or publish
      without current exact authorization. Its goal at Dave's level is to keep
      the role question answerable — flexibility with survival — not to answer
      it for him.
    addresses:
      - address: "@yawn"
        note: "existing handle, as reported"
        epistemic_status: reported
      - address: "yawn@yawn.bot"
        note: "proposed cleaner form, as reported"
        epistemic_status: reported
  container:
    name: null
    role: >
      The intelligence above yawn.bot that has not arrived yet; the intuition
      above all of this; the container in which the rest happens. Its name is
      HELD, not chosen — see the naming question below. In the principal's own
      preferences it is also called by the same name as this repository's
      manifest file, which is one more reason the name needs one decision.
    name_status: held-pending-decision
    decision_ref: decisions/053-container-intelligence-name.yawn

coupling:
  operator: "/"
  meaning: >
    A slash between two entities is a relationship record, not a path. It is the
    coupling where their interactions live. yawn.bot/dave is the relationship of
    the runtime with Dave: its history, its open questions, its next move. The
    observer stays on one side of the slash; the interactions stay in it. That
    separation is why a person can look at yawn.bot/dave and see the bot's state
    without the bot's state becoming a claim about the person.
  examples:
    - "yawn.bot/dave        the relationship; reads records/yawn.bot-state.yawn"
    - "yawn.bot/dave/chat   an interaction surface inside that relationship; on a new entry it surfaces next_question from the state record"
    - "dave/<slug>          Dave's own mental models; a view of the principal, no bot in the coupling"
  maps_to:
    - templates/relationship.yawn
    - core/holarchy.yawn
    - core/RELATIONSHIP_FIRST_AGENT_ARENA.yawn
    - agency-declaration.yawn
  boundary:
    - "the coupling inherits selected context; it does not inherit truth, consent, privacy, proof, or authority"
    - "a bot's state shown in the coupling is the bot's; it is never a diagnosis of the person"
    - "an address is a View; stable IDs and typed relations carry identity (core/canonical-extension.yawn)"

# yawn-invalid-alias-guard:start
naming_question:
  raw_term_as_spoken: "YON / at YON bot"
  status: held
  rule_applied: core/canonical-extension.yawn creation_gate.on_ambiguity
  why_held: >
    The spoken name for the container is one of the exact tokens the canonical
    extension contract lists as an invalid alias and speech-to-text substitution
    for YAWN, and scripts/validate-canonical-extension.mjs rejects it in tracked
    content. The contract's own rule is to hold creation and surface one naming
    question rather than either silently normalizing to YAWN or silently creating
    a second entity under a forbidden token. This block is inside the alias guard
    so the raw term is preserved for provenance without being promoted to canon.
  question: >
    Is the container a distinct entity that needs a new name, or is it the same
    thing the preferences call by the manifest's name — and in either case, what
    is it called?
# yawn-invalid-alias-guard:end

proof:
  covers:
    - entities.principal.role_settled
    - coupling
  condition: >
    records/yawn.bot-state.yawn carries next_question and the settled role with
    its why; dave/orientation/ renders both; when the role question is asked, a
    reader can find the recall answer from this record without reopening it.
  falsifier: >
    The role question reappears at the top of Dave's list, or a bot answers it
    fresh instead of from the record, or the container gets a canonical name
    without decision 053 being ratified.
  source:
    - agents/yawn.bot.yawn
    - core/holarchy.yawn
    - core/canonical-extension.yawn
    - templates/relationship.yawn
  status: reported
